window.__labLoaded({"id":"0.2","title":"Types of Data","goal":"Build a small student table with a nominal, an ordinal, a discrete and a continuous column, and see which summaries make sense for each.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Build a table with four kinds of data","text":"One column per type: eye colour (nominal), grade level (ordinal, so we tell pandas the order), siblings (discrete, counted) and height (continuous, measured). The dtypes show how pandas stores each one.","code":"import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    'eye': ['brown','blue','brown','green','brown','blue','hazel','brown','green','blue'],\n    'grade': ['Sophomore','Senior','Freshman','Junior','Sophomore','Senior','Freshman','Sophomore','Junior','Senior'],\n    'siblings': [1, 0, 2, 3, 1, 1, 0, 2, 4, 1],\n    'height_cm': [172.4, 165.1, 180.3, 158.9, 175.6, 169.8, 162.2, 177.0, 171.3, 183.5],\n})\ndf['eye'] = df['eye'].astype('category')\ndf['grade'] = pd.Categorical(df['grade'],\n    categories=['Freshman','Sophomore','Junior','Senior'], ordered=True)\nprint(df.dtypes)","out":"eye          category\ngrade        category\nsiblings        int64\nheight_cm     float64\ndtype: object","figs":[]},{"title":"Count the categories","text":"For categories the natural summary is a count (or a percentage), not an average. value_counts does it. With sort=False the ordinal column stays in its natural order, from Freshman to Senior.","code":"print(df['eye'].value_counts())\nprint()\nprint(df['grade'].value_counts(sort=False))\nprint()\nprint('share Junior or above:', (df['grade'] >= 'Junior').mean())","out":"eye\nbrown    4\nblue     3\ngreen    2\nhazel    1\nName: count, dtype: int64\n\ngrade\nFreshman     2\nSophomore    3\nJunior       2\nSenior       3\nName: count, dtype: int64\n\nshare Junior or above: 0.5","figs":[]},{"title":"Describe the numbers","text":"Counts and measurements are real amounts, so mean, standard deviation and quartiles all make sense. describe gives them in one table.","code":"print(df[['siblings', 'height_cm']].describe().round(2))","out":"       siblings  height_cm\ncount     10.00      10.00\nmean       1.50     171.61\nstd        1.27       7.88\nmin        0.00     158.90\n25%        1.00     166.28\n50%        1.00     171.85\n75%        2.00     176.65\nmax        4.00     183.50","figs":[]},{"title":"Why averaging a category makes no sense","text":"Pandas refuses to average a category. If you force it by turning grades into codes 1 to 4, you get a number like 2.6 that is not a grade, and it pretends the gaps between grades are equal. For an ordinal column use counts, the maximum or a share instead.","code":"for col in ['eye', 'grade']:\n    try:\n        df[col].mean()\n    except TypeError:\n        print(f'mean of {col}: not allowed (TypeError)')\n\ncodes = df['grade'].cat.codes + 1\nprint('mean of grade codes:', codes.mean(), '(is that a grade? no)')\nprint('highest grade present:', df['grade'].max())","out":"mean of eye: not allowed (TypeError)\nmean of grade: not allowed (TypeError)\nmean of grade codes: 2.6 (is that a grade? no)\nhighest grade present: Senior","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"eye <- factor(c('brown','blue','brown','green','brown','blue','hazel','brown','green','blue'))\ngrade <- factor(c('Sophomore','Senior','Freshman','Junior','Sophomore','Senior','Freshman','Sophomore','Junior','Senior'),\n  levels = c('Freshman','Sophomore','Junior','Senior'), ordered = TRUE)\nsiblings <- c(1, 0, 2, 3, 1, 1, 0, 2, 4, 1)\nheight_cm <- c(172.4, 165.1, 180.3, 158.9, 175.6, 169.8, 162.2, 177.0, 171.3, 183.5)\ntable(eye)\ntable(grade)\nsummary(siblings)\nsummary(height_cm)\nmean(eye)","expect":"eye: blue 3, brown 4, green 2, hazel 1; grade: Freshman 2, Sophomore 3, Junior 2, Senior 3; siblings mean 1.5; height_cm mean 171.61; mean(eye) gives NA with a warning","note":"R lists the eye colours alphabetically, while pandas lists them by size. mean(eye) returns NA with a warning, R's way of refusing to average a category."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Frequencies (categories) and Descriptives (numbers)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / eye (A6) grade siblings height.\nBEGIN DATA\nbrown 2 1 172.4 blue 4 0 165.1 brown 1 2 180.3 green 3 3 158.9 brown 2 1 175.6\nblue 4 1 169.8 hazel 1 0 162.2 brown 2 2 177.0 green 3 4 171.3 blue 4 1 183.5\nEND DATA.\nVALUE LABELS grade 1 'Freshman' 2 'Sophomore' 3 'Junior' 4 'Senior'.\nVARIABLE LEVEL eye (NOMINAL) /grade (ORDINAL) /siblings height (SCALE).\nFREQUENCIES VARIABLES=eye grade.\nDESCRIPTIVES VARIABLES=siblings height.","note":"SPSS stores grade as codes 1 to 4 with labels. VARIABLE LEVEL tells SPSS each column's measurement level; use Frequencies for the two categories and Descriptives for the two numbers."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset obs 10\ngenerate eye = word(\"brown blue brown green brown blue hazel brown green blue\", _n)\ngenerate grade = real(word(\"2 4 1 3 2 4 1 2 3 4\", _n))\ngenerate siblings = real(word(\"1 0 2 3 1 1 0 2 4 1\", _n))\ngenerate height = real(word(\"172.4 165.1 180.3 158.9 175.6 169.8 162.2 177.0 171.3 183.5\", _n))\nlabel define g 1 \"Freshman\" 2 \"Sophomore\" 3 \"Junior\" 4 \"Senior\"\nlabel values grade g\ntabulate eye\ntabulate grade\nsummarize siblings height","note":"Stata keeps the ordinal grade as codes 1 to 4 with value labels. tabulate counts categories, and summarize is for numbers only."},"try":["Add a new column 'satisfaction' with the ordered categories Poor, OK, Good and ask for its value_counts. Which summary would you report?","Change one height to 250.0 and run describe again. Which numbers move, and which do not?"]});
